In article <33630@mimsy.umd.edu> leonard@mimsy.umd.edu (Leonard Dickens) writes:

>2) Keep items you are collecting in the same state of identification.  For
>	example, say you are collecting identify scrolls to bless them.  Even
>	if you got a free identify (say from a throne) you should *not* use
>	it on the scrolls, because then all new identify scrolls you find
>	will not be lumped with them!

Uh, no. Once you know what something is, because you identified it or
because you used it, then instead of "you see here a scroll ELBIB YLOH"
you get "you see here a scroll of identify". Now, sometimes you know
what something is (eg a ring of conflict) but it hasn't been identified
and is still in your inventory as "a jade ring". Use the name command
to name all jade rings as conflict, then when you find another it
will be "you see here a jade ring called conflict".

One thing that is relevant to the blessing issue is that you can have
6 kinds of an item: known blessed, known uncursed, known cursed, and
unknown B,U,C. You can abuse this: hmmm, why didn't that scroll get
put with the other one? They obviously aren't BOTH uncursed... The only 
way to get them to lump together is to have the game know that you know 
that they are blessed/cursed. Just because your dog wouldn't step on it, 
it won't change inventory slots. Basically that means you need an altar 
for most effective blessing.

And remember, never drop your water on an altar to see if it's holy
water or not!  :-)

